mmarting621@gmail.com wrote:
>
> My choice of sokoban vs mines is based primarily upon my needs. If I
> need a food supply, I head to sokoban in such a manner as to generate
> each level, but with as little combat as possible.

Interesting.  I do the mines a level at a time and I take a corpse up
the stairs to eat it. That way I've never hungry and I can save every
food item found in Sokoban for later in the game.  Combining those two I
never lack food for the rest of the game.

> Once I solve sokoban or have sufficient wands/rings to journey into the
> mines, I do so. I seldom grab the prize at this point because I am not
> ready for the zoo.

In a lot of games I cycle up and down levels of the mines until I clear
each level.  Then when I encounter mine town I check what monsters are
there and decide - mine town or sokoban next.  I do both before going
below mine town.

> To me, the mines mean combat.

And as such XP levels for both me and my pet.  I try to balence them
about the same number of kills for each of us.

> Gnomes and dwarves are far too numerous as a source of food and items
> for me to ignore, and as such I almost never choose dwarf or gnome as
> a race.

As either of those I attempt to kill all other species and have my pet
focus on my own species.  It's a challenge.  I've also learned how to
limit the alignment damage of killing peacefuls by counting the number
of hostiles I kill after each peaceful before I kill the next one.

> but I do spend about 1/4 of my total game turns running between those
> branches, collecting holy water, b/u/c testing, etc until I have what I
> need to go further.

Yep.  It's a lot of preparation but by the time I've finished mines end
I'm nearly ready to face Medusa.  So most of my characters after that
die of typo or other dumb mistake not by getting bogged down in combat
other than on astral.

> Not sure if that answered your question, but I felt that sharing my
